The 1x1 conv's accumulators were in memory. Putting them in registers is worth 3x --
and the op class still loses
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), native Windows, mlir-aie ironenv + XRT SDK.
NPU and CPU arms measured in the same sitting. Witnesses below.

New code, all in this repository, nothing outside it modified:
  kernels/conv_accum/aie2/conv2dk1.cc       local copy + the accumulator peel
  kernels/conv_accum/aie2/conv2dk1_skip.cc  same change, the pipeline's third stage
  kernels/conv_accum/build_conv_accum.py    in-process source redirect + A/B driver

Commands:
  python kernels/conv_accum/build_conv_accum.py --cache-home <dir-A> --stock \
      --rest --shapes 32x32,64x32,128x32,256x32,512x32,56x56 --iters 30
  python kernels/conv_accum/build_conv_accum.py --cache-home <dir-B> \
      --rest --shapes ... --iters 30
  python kernels/bottleneck_sweep/cpu_sweep.py --shapes ...        (resnet_env)

THE DEFECT, EXACTLY. results/aie/conv_issue_rate_decomposed.log located the open int8 conv's
gap to the vendor DPU as issue rate, with the 1x1's hot loop the worst of it at 0.045 MACs per
cycle against the int8 GEMM's 0.889. The cause is one declaration. conv2dk1_i8_vector holds

    MMUL4x8x8 acc_tmp[4];
    for (int x = 0; x < n; x++) acc_tmp[x].mac(in_a, in_b);

where n is a RUNTIME value. Registers cannot be dynamically addressed, so the array is forced
to memory and every .mac() becomes load-four-quarters / mac / store-four-quarters.

THE CHANGE. Peel the n == 4 case into four NAMED accumulators -- the pattern mm.cc's
matmul_vectorized_2x2_mmul already uses. The array loop is kept as the tail and IS REACHED BY
A SHAPE IN THIS SWEEP: total_chunks is iw/4, so iw=56 gives 14 chunks = 4+4+4+2, and the 56x56
rows below run the tail at n=2 and verify. (iw=36 and iw=40 likewise give 9 and 10.) Four live
4x8x8 acc32 accumulators is one full 1024-bit register each, under this machine's measured
five-register spill-free ceiling.

THE PREDICTION THIS TESTS, AND IT IS FALSIFIABLE. results/aie/gemm_reblock_h11_npu.log
improved the int8 GEMM's kernel by 12.5% -- every spill gone, a full MAC every cycle -- and the
wall clock did not move, because that design is bound by a per-buffer delivery floor. The conv
sits at 4.5% of peak rather than 41.9%, so it should be genuinely issue-bound and should
actually speed up. If it did not, the conv would be delivery-bound too and the op class would
stay closed for a more interesting reason than anyone had recorded.

