--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. READING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PREDICTION HELD, AND THAT IS THE POINT. H11 improved the GEMM's inner loop by 12.5% and
moved the wall clock by nothing, inside this machine's own +/-5% drift. The same class of
change to the conv -- accumulators into registers, issue rate up 6.29x in the hot loop -- moves
marginal throughput by 2.99x and 3.01x across two series. The two designs are bound by
different things, and this is the first measurement in this repo that shows it by intervening
rather than by modelling. The conv was issue-bound; the GEMM was not.

EVERY SHAPE IMPROVED, AND THE FIT IS CLEAN. Marginal GOPS -- the shape-free slope, which is the
honest metric because per-point GOPS must rise with size regardless of the array -- goes

    series A    117.1 -> 350.3    2.99x     r^2 0.99987 / 0.99883
    series B    115.6 -> 348.4    3.01x     r^2 0.99498 / 0.99867

Both series, same sign, same magnitude, well outside the ~5% drift this machine shows between
repeats of an identical build. The fixed cost inside the hardware bracket also fell (341.5 ->
284.6 us in A, 456.3 -> 252.2 in B), but those intercepts are the noisiest quantity in the fit
and the change in them is not claimed.

AND THE OP CLASS STILL LOSES. This is the verdict, and the CPU baseline is named:
onnxruntime 1.22.1, ORT CPU EP, QDQ int8 reaching VNNI, same six shapes, same sitting, two
series -- 839.5 and 823.7 marginal GOPS.

    stock NPU     115.6-117.1 GOPS     CPU wins 7.1-7.2x
    patched NPU   348.4-350.3 GOPS     CPU wins 2.4x
    CPU           823.7-839.5 GOPS

A 3x kernel improvement moves the deficit from 7.2x to 2.4x and does not close it. The op-class
verdict does NOT reopen. What changes is the reason: it was "the kernel uses 5% of its issue
slots", and it is now "even with the issue slots used, one column of this array does not reach
a VNNI-equipped Zen4".

The CPU figure is consistent with what this repo already published: docs/SILICON.md 2.3 lists
819.0 GOPS for the same sweep, against 823.7-839.5 here. So the two arms of the comparison are
both anchored -- the CPU to a prior measurement, the NPU to its own stock arm in this sitting.

Against the vendor DPU, in the units docs/SILICON.md 2.3 uses (marginal rate for one column,
and the derived per-core split -- a column is four cores):

                                  one column     per core
  VitisAI DPU, yolov8l              1650 GOPS    412 GOPS
  open bottleneck, patched           350 GOPS     88 GOPS
  open bottleneck, stock (here)      116 GOPS     29 GOPS

The gap to the vendor narrows from ~14x against this sitting's stock arm to ~4.7x. Most of it
was this one defect; a real remainder is left, and conv2dk3 is the obvious place to look for
it.

A DISCREPANCY THAT HAS TO BE STATED, BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE A CONTRADICTION.
results/aie/bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log published 146.1 marginal GOPS for the stock
kernel, and docs/SILICON.md 2.3 quotes it. THIS SITTING'S STOCK ARM MEASURES 115.6-117.1 at the
same shapes. The kernel is not the same code: that sweep predates the 2026-09-07 width fix,
which rewrote conv2dk1_i8_vector to walk the width in blocks of N<=4 chunks -- the very array
whose spill this log removes -- where upstream had used eight concurrent accumulators. The
published run also could not compile 56x56 at all, and this one can, so the shape sets differ
too. INFERENCE, not measurement: the width fix appears to have cost ~20% at w=32 while making
non-multiple-of-32 widths correct. It was never measured at the time. The A/B above is
like-for-like -- both arms are the current kernel, same sitting, same shapes -- so the 2.99x is
unaffected by this; but 350.3 should be read against 146.1 as "2.4x the last published figure",
not against a number measured on different code.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- One design, one column, int8, one machine, one power mode, one sitting. Six shapes, all on
  the h-axis except 56x56; the w=64 shapes still fail to compile in BOTH arms (a memtile
  buffer limit, unrelated to this change) and are excluded from both fits.
- The host-load witness reads PEER, not CLEAR: another session's CPU quantization producer
  took about a core partway through. The NPU had no foreign context throughout. The CPU arm
  was run twice and agrees to 1.9% (839.5 and 823.7), and contention can only DEPRESS the CPU
  figure, which makes the "CPU still wins by 2.4x" verdict conservative rather than flattered.
- conv2dk3 -- the 3x3 middle stage -- was NOT touched. conv_issue_rate_decomposed.log measured
  it at 0.222 MACs/cycle and attributed the loss to vshift window alignment, a different
  defect needing a different fix. With both 1x1 stages now fast, the 3x3 is the likeliest new
  rate-limiter, and nothing here establishes where the remaining 2.4x to the CPU sits.
- The MAC issue rates are static ceilings read off the schedule, not counter measurements. No
  trace or hardware performance counter was run; 0.045 and 0.286 are bundles-and-vmac
  arithmetic, and the loops' trip counts are not weighted into a whole-kernel figure. In
  particular the whole-kernel speedup (2.99x) is smaller than the hot-loop issue-rate gain
  (6.29x), as it must be -- the loop is not all of the kernel.
- Correctness is the sweep's own gate, and it is a TOLERANCE check, not a bit-exact one:
  np.allclose(rtol=0, atol=INP_SCALE) against the torch int8 golden mlir-aie's test.py uses.
  Every shape in every arm reads ok=yes. That bounds the peel's error by one input quantum on
  these shapes; it is not a proof of exactness, and not a proof for inputs not tested.
- The w=32 shapes exercise ONLY the peeled n == 4 path (total_chunks = 8 = 4+4). The array
  tail is reached only by 56x56 (14 = 4+4+4+2), which verifies in both arms and both series.
  So the tail is covered by one shape, not by the sweep as a whole.
- The upstream pre-width-fix kernel was NOT built as a third arm, so the ~20% attribution
  below is inference from one changed file, not a measurement. The machine had a peer
  producer holding ~13 GB and free RAM at zero for the whole window in which that arm could
  have been built, and contending with another session's job was not worth a nice-to-have.
- The intercept changes are reported but not claimed; r^2 is high on the slope, and the
  intercept is where a six-point fit is weakest (series B's stock intercept, 456.3 us, differs
  from series A's 341.5 us on identical code).
